        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "457119fca51af5a2925eafba3b66891830708ef0",
            "parents": [
                "793bce1895f015ae27a0ce5fa4c75e38c2dadfd6"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/08/88608/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1774369981,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "fwiw, it seems that the commit is implying they are \"non-standard\".  The \"HTTP2-Settings\" one is listed by the IANA as deprecated, but I don't know how you do HTTP2 without it.  The \"Content-Transfer-Encoding\" one I can't find documented anywhere, other than maybe a really old RFC."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I can't really reconcile their commit message with their diff.\n\nContent-Transfer-Encoding I think is actually just a bug in our code, and should probably just be omitted.  It was originally added to prevent executable injection attacks, but browsers have a better way to do that these days, Redfish added base64 inline in the log entry, so we could probably just drop the base64 stuff entirely.\n\nHTTP2-Settings, I have no idea why boost removed it.  Mozilla doesn't list it on the page linked\nhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers\n\nBut iana does\nhttps://www.iana.org/assignments/http-fields/http-fields.xhtml\n\nI can dig deeper later."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "The commit message in beast is pretty worthless; no more than a few words.  If this is what the latest maintainers are going to do as standard practice, maybe we should reconsidering using it... It seemed that they tried to filter out any headers that weren't considered \"standard\" and left URLs buried in comments in as to where they obtained \"standard\" from.\n\nI'm fine if we merge this as-is since it doesn't actually change behavior.  But, I think it might be pointing out potential problems.\n\nHTTP2-Settings on IANA says \"obsolete\".  This might be pointing to some minor changes we need to do?\n\n> HTTP2-Settings\tobsoleted\t\t[RFC 7540, Section 3.2.1: Hypertext Transfer Protocol Version 2 (HTTP/2)]\tObsolete; see Section 11.1 of [RFC9113]\n\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html#section-11.1\n\nAgree Content-Transfer-Encoding can be removed later if desired."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 12,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> The commit message in beast is pretty worthless; no more than a few words.  If this is what the latest maintainers are going to do as standard practice, maybe we should reconsidering using it... It seemed that they tried to filter out any headers that weren't considered \"standard\" and left URLs buried in comments in as to where they obtained \"standard\" from.\n\nIt's not clear what happened here.  From my perspective this seems to be the exception not the rule, but if someone wants to go hunt down another http1.2 parser and do the engineering, I wouldn't be against it.  We changed once for security issues in the hand-rolled crow parser, no reason we couldn't do that again.\n\nAlternatively we could come up with our own Fields implementation and inject it into the existing parser and isolate ourselves from this.  At one point I looked at doing this anyway to drop the binary size of all the header strings it supports, but didn't seem worth the maintenance burden.\n\n> \n> I'm fine if we merge this as-is since it doesn't actually change behavior.  But, I think it might be pointing out potential problems.\n> \n> HTTP2-Settings on IANA says \"obsolete\".  This might be pointing to some minor changes we need to do?\n\nI'm not sure how to reconcile that with the actual RFC, which specifies this header as a \"MUST\"\nhttps://httpwg.org/specs/rfc7540.html#rfc.section.3.2.1\n\nI guess it must be because h2c upgrades are deprecated on the whole?\n\n> \n> > HTTP2-Settings\tobsoleted\t\t[RFC 7540, Section 3.2.1: Hypertext Transfer Protocol Version 2 (HTTP/2)]\tObsolete; see Section 11.1 of [RFC9113]\n> \n> https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html#section-11.1\n> \n> Agree Content-Transfer-Encoding can be removed later if desired."
                }
            ],
